---
import PageLayout from '~/layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import Divider from '~/components/Divider.astro';

const subject = encodeURIComponent('Application: Software Developer who enjoys teaching');
const body = encodeURIComponent(
  `Hi everyone codes team,

I'd like to apply for the mentor role.

Short answers to the questions on the job ad:

1. Do you speak English and German fluently? Are you comfortable teaching in either language?


2. Are you available on-site in Vienna for at least 3 days per week from 8:30 to 17:00?


3. Do you have any formal qualifications as a teacher (prior teaching experience, trainer certificate, etc.)?


4. Why do you want to be a mentor at everyone codes?
   (Please write informally and from the heart — no AI-generated text)


CV is attached.

Best regards,`
);
const applyMailto = `mailto:people@everyonecodes.io?subject=${subject}&body=${body}`;
---

<PageLayout
  title="Software Developer who enjoys teaching (f/m/d) – everyone codes"
  description="Join everyone codes as a programming mentor and help career switchers start their journey in IT. 20-30h/week, Vienna, immediately."
>
  <article class="job-page">
    <section class="page-hero">
      <div class="container">
        <h1>Software Developer who enjoys teaching <span class="fmd">(f/m/d)</span></h1>
        <p class="meta">20 to 30 hours per week &nbsp;·&nbsp; immediately &nbsp;·&nbsp; Vienna</p>
        <a href={applyMailto} class="btn-primary">Apply now</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <Divider />

    <section class="content-section">
      <div class="inner">
        <h2>About everyone codes</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>everyone codes</strong> is a non-profit that offers high quality
          programming courses to career switchers, for free (we're sponsored by the
          government, but highly independent).
        </p>

        <h3>What do our programming courses look like?</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>We believe that programming is something you learn mainly by doing it yourself</li>
          <li>Thus, participants spend most of their time on programming exercises that we designed</li>
          <li>They also receive input through lectures, live coding sessions and Q&amp;A sessions</li>
          <li>They get a detailed code review with line-by-line feedback on some of their code</li>
          <li>The entire course is designed to simulate a real job situation as much as possible</li>
          <li>Our courses work — from our previous intake that finished in 2024, 15 of our 19 graduates have found a job, despite the tough market conditions. Earlier placement numbers were even higher</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Who and what do we teach?</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Our current courses target complete beginners (general computer users)</li>
          <li>Courses are 100% free for participants, their cost of living is also covered for the duration of the course</li>
          <li>Our main course is full-time for participants and has a duration of ~10 months</li>
          <li>We have a difficult multi-stage application process for participants, ensuring we get extremely motivated learners</li>
          <li>There are about 15–20 participants per course, and we get around 100 applicants</li>
          <li>We currently teach mainly Java, since it allows easy entry in the Austrian job market. We plan on introducing other languages in the future. We're <strong>not</strong> only looking for Java Developers, so keep reading!</li>
          <li>We start with programming basics (variables, functions, loops…) and work our way to very basic backend web development. At the end of the course, participants are able to write basic web apps, with a few REST endpoints and a database connection. They'll also learn unix basics and general tools like git, Postman and Docker</li>
          <li>We help our participants find a job afterwards by connecting them with companies and helping them with mock interviews and other soft skills training</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>How do we ensure quality?</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>In many other teaching positions, they don't pay you for any prepwork or grading. At everyone codes, prepwork is part of your regular work hours, and you have a lot of autonomy over this process</li>
          <li>Autonomy is extremely high in general. While you can't completely change the entire curriculum (some details, like the programming language for example, must remain the same), you can re-design exercises, slides, etc. so they fit your teaching style</li>
          <li>While we do use some online materials, the majority of our materials were created by ourselves</li>
          <li>A lot of other courses promise the moon, but never actually deliver — anyone can claim to teach Frontend, Backend and DevOps in 3 months. We don't believe that is possible. We ensure that participants have enough time to <em>actually</em> learn and properly understand the content</li>
          <li>During the courses, there are very few graded assignments, reducing discussions about grades to a minimum</li>
          <li>Participants are graded on actual code they wrote — for graded assignments, they can use their own machine with internet access — just like in a real job</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <Divider />

    <section class="content-section">
      <div class="inner">
        <h2>About the role</h2>
        <p>
          We're looking for an additional Software Developer / Programming Mentor to
          join us long-term.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>This role is for 20–30h per week, depending on your needs</li>
          <li>There will be an onboarding phase, where you will shadow a mentor and learn from them how our current courses are structured</li>
          <li>You'll also have some time to prepare your own lectures and materials if you want</li>
          <li>As your onboarding progresses, you'll gradually take over entire weeks and months of individual courses. How much you'll take over will depend on how many hours you are available</li>
          <li>You'll also be substituting other mentors when they're on vacation/sick leave</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>This is what your day-to-day would look like:</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Do interactive lectures, live coding and Q&amp;A sessions, both online and in-person</li>
          <li>Design programming exercises, lecture slides and other education content</li>
          <li>Do code reviews and discuss mistakes with participants</li>
          <li>Be part of the application process for the next batch of participants</li>
          <li>Exchange with other mentors</li>
          <li>Support the team where needed — across project management, employer outreach, and soft skills training for participants (e.g. mock interviews, accompanying participants to events like career fairs, organizing events like coding contests)</li>
          <li>We're a very small team, so we expect you to be comfortable wearing many hats and filling in wherever needed</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <Divider />

    <section class="content-section">
      <div class="inner">
        <h2>What we offer</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Autonomy to do your job in a flat hierarchy and room for personal development</li>
          <li>The chance to directly support career switchers and to produce a positive social impact — for many of our participants, the courses and job opportunities they have afterwards are life changing</li>
          <li>A motivated and multicultural team with a positive working atmosphere</li>
          <li>We ensure that everyone on the team gets enough meeting-free time to do focused, distraction-free work</li>
          <li>We understand that time spent at the office doesn't necessarily equate to productivity. Work smarter, not harder. The most important thing is that the job gets done</li>
          <li>Flexible working hours — we basically don't care when you work, as long as the work you committed to gets done (and we make sure you don't over-commit)</li>
          <li>While you'll have to be present for any in-person sessions that you lead in the course, you generally have unlimited home office during times where you're not in the course. For this role, you can expect 3 in-person days for course hours (8:30am to 5pm) at the office per week</li>
          <li>We do have a very nice, air-conditioned office in Vienna near U4 Heiligenstadt</li>
          <li>A flashy job title for your resume (we can decide together on it)</li>
          <li>We offer either regular employment (this is the usual arrangement) or freelance contracting hours, depending on your availability</li>
          <li>For legal reasons, we have to state the minimum salary for this position, which is <strong>3064€ gross for 40h/week</strong>. Your actual salary will of course be higher, based on your skills and experience. Let's talk!</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <Divider />

    <section class="content-section">
      <div class="inner">
        <h2>Your profile</h2>
        <p>
          As you'll see below, we don't have a long list of hard requirements. We care
          much more about mindset and culture fit. If you're someone who genuinely enjoys
          programming, teaching and learning, we think you can acquire any missing hard
          skills while you're with us.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Fluent in English and German. While English is the main company language, our clients vastly prefer German so unfortunately, fluent German is a hard requirement for this role</strong></li>
          <li>Programming skills: You should be confident in your programming skills in at least one major language. It doesn't have to be Java. We don't want to put any more hard requirements on this, but we will have a screening for this in the interviewing process</li>
          <li>
            Teaching skills: You should <strong>really enjoy</strong> teaching. You know how to explain difficult technical concepts to complete beginners, or are eager to learn it
            <ul>
              <li>Patience and empathy for learners, taking your time to explain things over and over if necessary</li>
              <li>A good feeling for educational pacing — you know how to break down complex topics into small, easily digestible steps</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li>You should enjoy working in a fairly small company and team</li>
          <li>You should be able to work highly independently and self-organized — e.g. you prepare well for lectures/teaching sessions</li>
          <li>The desire for continuous personal and professional development</li>
          <li>The desire to make a positive contribution to society</li>
          <li>Interested in teaching in-person in Vienna for at least 3 days per week</li>
          <li>Prior experience in an educational setting is not a strict requirement, but a huge advantage</li>
          <li>Empathic communication and conflict resolution skills both in a 1-on-1 and group setting</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <Divider />

    <section class="content-section">
      <div class="inner">
        <h2>Application process</h2>
        <p>
          The entire process should take just a little over 2 hours of your time and take
          less than 4 weeks from first contact to offer.
        </p>

        <div class="rounds">
          <div class="round">
            <h3>Round 1 — Phone Screen <span class="round-time">(30 min)</span></h3>
            <ul>
              <li>After you send in your CV (see below), we'll set up a short phone pre-screen with our CTO, Alex (max. 30 min)</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="round">
            <h3>Round 2 — Online Live Coding Challenge <span class="round-time">(30 min)</span></h3>
            <ul>
              <li>After the phone screen, you'll hear back from us, inviting you to the live coding challenge</li>
              <li>The interview will be with our CTO via video call</li>
              <li>Will take max 30 minutes</li>
              <li>Includes a short technical challenge where you will write code in your own editor. Ideally you'll use Java, since it is what we teach, but if you're more comfortable in another language that is also great. No prep needed</li>
              <li>You receive extensive feedback after the session</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="round">
            <h3>Round 3 — On-Site <span class="round-time">(1h)</span></h3>
            <ul>
              <li>After the Live Coding Challenge, you'll hear back from us, inviting you to the final interview round</li>
              <li>Preferably on-site but can also be via video call</li>
              <li>Will take max 1h</li>
              <li>There will be a short teaching challenge</li>
              <li>You will meet our CEO, Edina, and our CTO, Alex in-person</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="round">
            <h3>Offer</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>Within a week, you'll hear from us and potentially receive an offer</li>
              <li>Once we agree to work together, you could start immediately (when our next course starts)</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <Divider />

    <section class="content-section apply-section">
      <div class="inner">
        <h2>Interested?</h2>
        <p>
          If you have any questions before applying, please don't hesitate to contact us
          at <a href="mailto:people@everyonecodes.io">people@everyonecodes.io</a>.
        </p>

        <p>
          To apply, please send your CV to
          <a href="mailto:people@everyonecodes.io">people@everyonecodes.io</a> and
          include a short answer to the following questions:
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li>Do you speak English and German fluently? Are you comfortable teaching in either language?</li>
          <li>Are you available on-site in Vienna for at least 3 days per week from 8:30 to 17:00?</li>
          <li>Do you have any formal qualifications as a teacher (prior teaching experience, trainer certificate, etc.)? This is not a strict requirement, but helps us with legal requirements we have towards the government, so it's a bonus.</li>
          <li>This is the most important one: Why do you want to be a mentor at everyone codes?</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          Please <strong>do not AI generate</strong> the answer to these questions,
          particularly the last one — write it informally and from the heart. You don't
          need to write a formal cover letter — just a regular email is fine.
        </p>

        <p>
          Bonus points if you include anything you've made in the past where you teach
          something. Could be anything — a blog post, a readme on GitHub, a video, some
          code documentation. If you don't have anything like that yet, don't worry, you
          can still apply!
        </p>

        <p>
          If you see this job ad on LinkedIn or another platform, please send a copy of
          your application also to the above email — all incoming applications will be
          processed via email.
        </p>

        <p class="dei">
          We are strongly committed to diversity and inclusion and explicitly welcome
          applications from women, non-binary individuals, and people from underrepresented
          groups.
        </p>

        <p class="closing">We're really looking forward to hearing from you!</p>

        <a href={applyMailto} class="btn-primary">Send your application</a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</PageLayout>

<style>
  .job-page {
    max-width: 100%;
  }
  .page-hero {
    min-height: 50vh;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    text-align: center;
    padding: 100px 24px 60px;
  }
  .container,
  .inner {
    max-width: 820px;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }
  .page-hero h1 {
    font-size: clamp(28px, 4.5vw, 48px);
    line-height: 1.15;
    margin-bottom: 16px;
    color: var(--white);
  }
  .fmd {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: 0.75em;
  }
  .meta {
    font-family: var(--font-heading);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 14px;
    color: var(--violet);
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    margin-bottom: 32px;
  }
  .btn-primary {
    display: inline-block;
    padding: 14px 34px;
    background: linear-gradient(90deg, var(--magenta) 0%, var(--violet) 100%);
    color: var(--white);
    font-family: var(--font-heading);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 14px;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    border-radius: 999px;
    transition: opacity 0.2s, transform 0.2s;
  }
  .btn-primary:hover,
  .btn-primary:focus-visible {
    opacity: 0.9;
    transform: translateY(-2px);
  }

  .content-section {
    padding: 56px 24px;
  }
  .content-section h2 {
    font-size: clamp(24px, 3vw, 32px);
    margin-bottom: 20px;
    color: var(--white);
  }
  .content-section h3 {
    font-size: 18px;
    color: var(--magenta);
    margin: 28px 0 14px;
  }
  .content-section p {
    font-size: 15px;
    line-height: 1.85;
    color: var(--text);
    margin-bottom: 16px;
  }
  .content-section ul {
    list-style: disc;
    padding-left: 22px;
    margin-bottom: 18px;
  }
  .content-section li {
    font-size: 14px;
    line-height: 1.8;
    color: var(--text);
    margin-bottom: 8px;
  }
  .content-section a {
    color: var(--violet);
    text-decoration: underline;
  }
  .content-section a:hover {
    color: var(--magenta);
  }

  .rounds {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 28px;
    margin-top: 28px;
  }
  .round {
    background: var(--card);
    border-radius: 10px;
    border-left: 4px solid var(--violet);
    padding: 24px 28px;
  }
  .round h3 {
    color: var(--white);
    margin: 0 0 12px;
    font-size: 17px;
  }
  .round-time {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: 0.85em;
  }

  .apply-section .dei {
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-top: 24px;
  }
  .apply-section .closing {
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--white);
    margin: 24px 0 32px;
  }
</style>
